<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="b28344b2">&lt;p&gt;
    The framework for testing a target solution is commonly organized into test levels and test types. Each &lt;em&gt;test
    level&lt;/em&gt; defines a target, which can be an entire system or any number of modules. Defining a test strategy typically
    consists of multiple levels where each level identifies the components of the solution under test. &lt;em&gt;Test types&lt;/em&gt;
    are performed at each level to fulfill a specific purpose or objective. Different types of tests are performed at each
    level to fulfill various objectives. In other words, different objectives are addressed at different test levels. Test
    levels identify the components of the target solution under test while defining test types assist in selecting which
    test scenarios and scripts in order to fulfill the test objective. &lt;em&gt;Test techniques&lt;/em&gt; offer a structured thought
    process to accomplish test objectives. Structuring a test effort using test levels, types and techniques lays the
    groundwork for defining how the target solution is tested.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Test levels
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    A test level is the object of testing. In other words, a test level represents the target of the test, which can vary
    from a single module, a system or the entire target solution comprised of many systems. Testing of a target solution
    usually has different layers containing a combination of unit, integration, system integration and user acceptance test
    levels.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Unit
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Unit testing validates the internal logic of the software solution by way of evaluating the solution by its smallest
    units. Unit tests are limited to the individual units and do not extend further. The purpose of unit testing is to
    verify that every executable statement executes successfully and achieves the desired results.  Unit testing is
    not intended to validate that end-to-end functionality is working correctly. The primary objective is to validate that
    the developed code has been thoroughly tested against defined requirements and behaves as expected. Unit tests are
    typically performed by developers at the conclusion of the development cycle.  Unit tests are based off of the
    detailed design for the solution.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Integration
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    In integration testing, software modules are combined and tested as a group. Integration testing aggregates unit tested
    modules and verifies the interaction between aggregated units behaves as expected. Top-down and bottom-up techniques
    can be applied in integration testing, especially when there is a hierarchically structured target solution.
    Integration testing typically occurs after unit test and before system integration testing. On large scale projects,
    incremental integration testing is commonly performed prior to assembling all the components at once.  Integration
    tests are based off of the global design for the solution.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    System integration
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    This stage of testing entails functional and non-functional testing of the entire target solution in one single test
    environment. A key difference between integration testing and system integration testing is that integration testing
    aggregates individual units while system integration testing (SIT) aggregates systems within the target solution. The
    objective of SIT is to identify and correct most of the significant defects so that user acceptance testing can be
    conducted and the code released with a high degree of assurance that it will meet both the requirement specifications.
    Typically, the bulk of the defects are identified during the SIT phase. This testing is driven primarily from the
    design. As a result, the SIT test cases will be traced back to system requirements, use cases, and design components to
    demonstrate the comprehensiveness of the testing. Typically included is all positive and negative functional testing,
    regression testing, performance testing, volume testing, stress testing, and any necessary interface testing to
    validate the quality of the integrated code. All significant aspects of new functionality should be tested. SIT is the
    most comprehensive test level and is typically conducted by an independent test team.  System integration tests
    are based off of the system requirements for the solution.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    User acceptance
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    In user acceptance testing (UAT), trained end users test the implemented solution to verify and confirm that the target
    solution meets the scope of business requirements and user expectations. These end users represent the business and
    have an understanding of the solution set or package which was used to satisfy the requirements. The test executes a
    number of critical end-to-end integration test scenarios and the success of acceptance depends upon the sign-off from
    the user acceptance team. Completing user acceptance testing is essential in assuring that the business has accepted
    and approved of the delivered system.  User acceptance tests are based off of the business requirements for the
    solution.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Test types
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Testing is performed to fulfill different test objectives. A test type represents the purpose or objective of testing
    on the target under test.  It expected that different types of tests will be performed at each level with
    some types of tests being applicable to multiple levels depending on the scope of what is being tested. 
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Regression
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Regression testing is performed to uncover defects or regressions in existing functional and non-functional areas of a
    target solution after introducing any changes to a test environment. The purpose of regression testing is to validate
    that existing components of the target solution have not been impacted by new changes. Regression tests can be manually
    or automatically performed and are periodically performed throughout all test levels.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Performance
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Performance testing is a general testing term to describe evaluation of system performance, which can be broken down
    into various attributes such as stress, volume, load, duration, configuration and scalability.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Load
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Load testing is the simplest form of performance testing. A load test is usually conducted to understand the behavior
    of the system under a specific expected load. This load can be the expected concurrent number of users on the
    application performing a specific number of transactions within the set duration. This test will give out the response
    times of all the important business critical transactions. If the database, application server, etc. are also
    monitored, then this simple test can itself point towards bottlenecks in the application software.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Stress
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Stress testing (also known as capacity testing) is normally used to understand the upper limits of capacity within the
    system. This kind of test is done to determine the system&apos;s robustness in terms of extreme load and helps application
    administrators to determine if the system will perform sufficiently if the current load goes well above the expected
    maximum.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Duration
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Duration testing is a form of performance testing designed to test software under different time durations using
    varying performance attributes (load, data, stress etc.) Duration testing is typically done for an extended period of
    time to identify issues (e.g., memory leaks).
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Security
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Security testing is performed at the application and system level. Application security, which includes security with
    respect to data and business functions, tests that users are restricted to specific roles, actions and data based on
    defined security requirements. System security is used so that only users granted access to the system are capable
    of accessing the applications and only through the appropriate gateways.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Data conversion
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    This testing involves three areas of verification. First, if the system requires data conversion, the conversion
    should be run and verified. Second, the data should be verified for test readiness, but also to check that the system
    is properly handling the data and changes to data. Finally, data integrity, in terms of the maintenance of the data
    through unexpected activity, errors and concurrent access to the data must be verified so that the system maintains
    data integrity, as defined by the product.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Functional
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    A type of testing conducted to evaluate the compliance of a system or component with specified functional requirements.
    Functional testing ignores the internal mechanism of a system or component and focuses solely on the outputs generated
    in response to selected inputs and execution conditions.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Disaster Recovery
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Recovery testing is aimed at verifying software and hardware restart capabilities after a system crash or other
    “disaster.”
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Configuration
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Configuration testing verifies the operation of the system under test on different software and hardware
    configurations. In most environments, the particular software and hardware specifications vary. For example, client
    workstations may have different software loaded (e.g. applications, drivers, etc.) and at any one time many different
    combinations may be active and using different resources. In general, configuration testing should be based on the
    hardware and software configuration defined in the target solution.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Connectivity
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Connectivity testing is designed to test connectivity between networked devices.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Smoke
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Smoke testing is a quick test (sometimes called a sanity check) often used to validate a new build in an environment.
    It validates that the configuration is available, functional, and no defects are apparent.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;
    Test techniques
&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Test techniques offer a systematic thought process to discover as many defects in the target solution as possible. Test
    techniques are employed to increase the likelihood of discovering defects and to act as an aid in fulfilling test
    objectives.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    White-box
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    In contract to black-box testing, white-box testing (also known as clear box testing, transparent testing, glass box
    testing and structural testing) tests the solution&apos;s internal logic and data structures instead of solution
    functionality. Programming skills are often required to design test scripts that traverse branches in the source code
    and determine expected outputs based on defined requirements. White-box testing can be applied to unit, integration,
    system integration and regression tests.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Black-box
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    In contrast to white-box testing, black-box testing tests solution functionality without any concern for internal logic
    and data structures. Given a set of input, black-box tests evaluate whether the expected output is produced and do not
    test how the solution arrived at the expected output. The advantage of this technique is that it focuses on testing
    aspects of the solution that are important to the user. The disadvantage is that poor coding techniques or inefficient
    design will not be identified by this type of testing. Black-box testing can be applied to unit, integration, system
    integration and user acceptance tests.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Top-down
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    An incremental testing approach that starts with the highest-level element of a hierarchy, and proceeds through
    progressively lower level elements, until complete. This technique is useful for integration and system integration
    test levels.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Bottom-up
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    When employing bottom-up testing, the lowest level components are tested first and then used to facilitate the
    testing of higher level components. The process is repeated until the component at the top of the hierarchy is tested.
    All the bottom or low-level modules, procedures or functions are integrated and then tested. After testing lower level
    integrated modules, the next level of modules will be formed and tested. This technique is helpful only when all or
    most of the modules of the same development level are ready. This method also helps to determine the levels of software
    developed and makes it easier to report testing progress in the form of a percentage. This technique is useful for
    integration and system integration test levels.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Dynamic
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Dynamic testing is normally are associated with the executable tests in the software development lifecycle. In dynamic
    tests, code is compiled and executed in order to examine whether a coded solution produces the expected output given a
    set of input. Dynamic testing is performed in all test levels and types where software is involved.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;
    Static
&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Static testing is typically a detailed manual comparison and examination of a work product’s characteristics to an
    expected set of attributes, experiences, and standards. Static testing is used to verify “non-executable” deliverables.
    Walk-throughs, inspections and reviews are examples of static testing.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
